# airlock: every machine-specific value, in one place.
#
#   cp install/config.env.example install/config.env
#   $EDITOR install/config.env
#
# install/install.sh and install/doctor.sh source install/config.env if it
# exists. It is gitignored. Nothing else in this repository hard-codes a home
# directory, a user name, or a Claude account directory -- if you find
# something that does, that is a bug.
#
# NO SECRET VALUE GOES IN THIS FILE. It records the PATH to the file that
# holds the API key, never the key itself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Path to a file containing a `TYPESAFE_API_KEY=...` line, mode 600.
# airlock/keyfile.py reads it with plain Python file I/O: never cat'd, never
# put on a command line, never logged. The full resolution order (including the
# keyfile.path pointer install.sh writes for the hook, which runs with a bare
# environment) is documented in ONE place: airlock/keyfile.py's module
# docstring. Read it there rather than inferring it from this file.
# Every component in the kit -- the guard, belay, compaction, docclass,
# logtriage, browser, review -- reads this same one key, so its default is
# kit-level and not under any single component's directory:
# ~/.config/jev-kit/env. The guard-era ~/.config/airlock/env is still resolved
# straight after it, for good, so an existing install needs no action.
# Leave this commented out on a fresh machine.
#AIRLOCK_KEY_FILE="$HOME/.config/jev-kit/env"

# Extra key-file paths to fall back on, colon-separated, for a machine that
# already kept its key somewhere else before adopting this layout. They are
# tried in order, only when neither AIRLOCK_KEY_FILE nor JEVKIT_KEY_FILE is set
# and neither default exists. Nothing is baked into the code, so a fresh
# machine leaves this unset.
#AIRLOCK_LEGACY_KEY_FILES="$HOME/.config/<your-org>/env"

# Extra paths R1 should treat as a secret store, colon-separated. Set this to
# the same paths as AIRLOCK_LEGACY_KEY_FILES so the guard still refuses to
# print a key file that lives outside the default locations. Both defaults are
# protected already.
#AIRLOCK_EXTRA_SECRET_PATHS="$HOME/.config/<your-org>/env"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which Claude account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A machine with one Claude login can leave these unset; they default to
# ~/.claude. A machine with several logins must say which one is being spent,
# because the tuning judge and the browser text model both run real sessions.
#
# The tuning loop's judge (Opus, by default, at high effort):
#AIRLOCK_TUNE_CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude"
# The A/B bench's sessions:
#AIRLOCK_BENCH_CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude"
# The claude-cli shim, and the browser component's text model, both read the
# ordinary CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:
#CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude"

# Override the `claude` binary if it is not on PATH for systemd user units:
#AIRLOCK_TUNE_CLAUDE_BIN="$HOME/.npm-global/bin/claude"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where airlock lives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployed releases and the `current` symlink that settings.json and the
# systemd units point at. Defaults to ~/.local/share/airlock (or the old
# ~/.local/share/jev-guard if only that exists).
#AIRLOCK_HOME="$HOME/.local/share/airlock"

# Config (mode, rules.json, the kill switch) and state (shadow log, loop
# protection, tuning state). Both fall back to the old jev-guard names when
# only those exist -- see airlock/paths.py.
#AIRLOCK_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.config/airlock"
#AIRLOCK_STATE_DIR="$HOME/.local/state/airlock"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard behaviour
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# shadow (log only, the default) | enforce (a deny blocks) | off.
# This is normally set in $AIRLOCK_CONFIG_DIR/mode rather than here, so it
# can be changed without reinstalling.
#AIRLOCK_MODE="shadow"

# Hard budget on each synchronous Jev call in enforce mode, milliseconds.
#AIRLOCK_BUDGET_MS="1500"

# Fraction of calls that cannot possibly deny but are judged anyway, so the
# tuning loop keeps seeing ordinary traffic.
#AIRLOCK_SAMPLE_RATE="0.05"

# Warm-connection daemon pool size.
#AIRLOCK_DAEMON_POOL="2"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional components
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where browser/install.sh clones browser-use/jev-ultrafast:
#AIRLOCK_BROWSER_DIR="$HOME/code/jev-ultrafast"

# Where review/install.sh clones devagrawal09/jev-review, and the review
# command that review/jev-prefilter.sh hands its --note to:
#AIRLOCK_REVIEW_DIR="$HOME/code/jev-review"
#AIRLOCK_REVIEW_GATE=""

# jevcal, used by tuning/calibrate.sh to pick and lock thresholds. Install it
# into its own venv; this is the path to the resulting binary.
#AIRLOCK_JEVCAL_BIN="$HOME/tools/jevcal/.venv/bin/jevcal"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Monitoring
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Uptime Kuma push URL is a capability token -- anyone holding it can push
# a fake "up" -- so it is treated like a secret and lives in the key file
# above, NOT here. monitoring/run_health_check.sh loads it from there.
# Referenced by name only:
#   AIRLOCK_KUMA_PUSH_URL
# The older GS_KUMA_AIRLOCK_PUSH_URL and GS_KUMA_JEV_PUSH_URL are still read,
# so a machine that already sets one needs no change.
